WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.460
Robert Townsend: How you doing?

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:02.794
Tracy Clayton:
I'm okay, how are you?

00:00:02.794 --> 00:00:04.046
Robert: Very good.

00:00:04.046 --> 00:00:05.297
Tracy: Oh, I'm sorry,
I stepped on your foot.

00:00:05.297 --> 00:00:06.548
Robert: Oh no, it's all good.

00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:07.841
Tracy: This is
going horribly already.

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.134
Robert: Oh, stop.

00:00:09.134 --> 00:00:10.802
Tracy: Please
have a seat, please.

00:00:10.802 --> 00:00:12.054
It is so
nice to meet you.

00:00:12.054 --> 00:00:13.430
Robert: Well,
thank you for having me.

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.265
Tracy: Thank
you for joining us.

00:00:15.265 --> 00:00:17.684
It's Robert Townsend,
oh my gosh.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.979
Okay, so you were born and
raised--or you were raised

00:00:20.979 --> 00:00:22.314
in Chicago.

00:00:22.314 --> 00:00:23.565
Robert: Yes.

00:00:23.565 --> 00:00:25.234
Tracy: What do you wish
that people knew about Chicago?

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:27.653
Robert: When I think of
Chicago, you know, I grew up

00:00:27.653 --> 00:00:31.073
in the hood, but I always
considered it glorious

00:00:31.073 --> 00:00:32.407
and beautiful.

00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:36.912
And you know, when people think
of it as nasty or the gangs

00:00:36.912 --> 00:00:41.250
and all of that, you know, yeah,
but I think in all, you know,

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.335
communities, you
have that other side.

00:00:43.335 --> 00:00:47.798
I think the misconception about
Chicago is that there's so much

00:00:47.798 --> 00:00:51.969
poverty and everything is wrong,
but there's a lot of beautiful

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:53.720
people in where I came from.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.724
You know, the neighborhood,
the village raised the village,

00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:59.101
you know what I mean?

00:00:59.101 --> 00:01:01.353
It takes a village to raise
a child, you know, and it's

00:01:01.353 --> 00:01:03.772
like there was a community,
everybody cared.

00:01:03.772 --> 00:01:05.065
And so, that's what
I really remember.

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:08.652
And I think that's what I would
really send a message and say,

00:01:08.652 --> 00:01:12.072
you know, don't judge a book by
its cover because you hear

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:13.282
about a few things.

00:01:13.282 --> 00:01:16.326
But I--my childhood, I just have
the best memories of growing up.

00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:19.204
And you know, when you don't
know what's in everybody else's

00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:21.873
refrigerator, you don't
know what you're missing.

00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:25.627
So, as a kid, you know,
I had no idea.

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:28.338
But we--you know, we had love
and we had God, and that was

00:01:28.338 --> 00:01:29.756
the most important thing.

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:32.426
Tracy: What was--what was
baby Robert like?

00:01:32.426 --> 00:01:34.052
Like, when you were like
younger, what kind of kid

00:01:34.052 --> 00:01:35.345
were you?

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:39.016
Robert: When I was a kid, I
was--because I lived in a rough

00:01:39.016 --> 00:01:42.769
neighborhood, I would rush home
from school, and all I did

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:44.187
was watch television.

00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:48.525
And so, back then when I was
like eight years old, you know,

00:01:48.525 --> 00:01:52.696
my nickname was TV Guide because
I watched so much television,

00:01:52.696 --> 00:01:54.865
I could tell you
everything about TV.

00:01:54.865 --> 00:01:58.744
So, I was this kid that saw
the world through television.

00:01:58.744 --> 00:02:00.829
Tracy: And you also
did Second City in Chicago?

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:05.125
Robert: Yes, I studied with
Jo Forsberg, improv'd with her,

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:07.669
and she was Bill Murray's
improv teacher.

00:02:07.669 --> 00:02:11.423
So, I was--you know, I was
a kid, you know, like 17, 18.

00:02:11.423 --> 00:02:16.011
And I was, you know, doing
theatre with Xback as well as

00:02:16.011 --> 00:02:17.512
studying at Second City.

00:02:17.512 --> 00:02:18.889
Tracy: What was
Second City like?

00:02:18.889 --> 00:02:20.098
Robert: You know what?

00:02:20.098 --> 00:02:23.685
I learned my manners as an actor
at Second City because what they

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:27.647
taught you was that if you can
improvise and create something

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:31.443
from nothing, when you have
the props, you could change

00:02:31.443 --> 00:02:32.861
the world.

00:02:32.861 --> 00:02:35.322
And so they--you know, you had
a chair, and that chair

00:02:35.322 --> 00:02:37.407
could be a rocket ship.

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:39.326
You know, so you just had
all these illusions

00:02:39.326 --> 00:02:40.869
and things that you created.

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:44.164
But I learned so much by
understanding the ability

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:45.415
to create with nothing.

00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:48.001
Tracy: So, how has the industry
changed since you

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.753
first started?

00:02:49.753 --> 00:02:51.088
Robert: Well, you know,
here's the thing.

00:02:51.088 --> 00:02:53.924
I think it's a different time in
Hollywood, it really is because

00:02:53.924 --> 00:02:56.676
there are more images.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:03:00.097
So, when you think in terms
of the characters that

00:03:00.097 --> 00:03:02.766
Shonda Rhimes has created,
you know,

00:03:02.766 --> 00:03:04.101
"How to Get Away With Murder,"

00:03:04.101 --> 00:03:08.230
"Scandal," when you think in
terms of the shows that are on

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:11.149
OWN like "Queen Sugar,"

00:03:11.149 --> 00:03:15.070
when you think in terms
of BET and,

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.240
you know, "The Quad"
and different shows,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.076
everybody--you know,
on HBO, "Insecure."

00:03:21.076 --> 00:03:24.371
There's a lot of new images
out there, there's new voices.

00:03:24.371 --> 00:03:28.458
And I think that
the game is changing.

00:03:28.458 --> 00:03:30.877
Is there room for more?

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:34.673
Yes, but the game is changing,
I think in a good way, though

00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:37.008
there is still baggage
from the old days.

00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:39.219
Tracy: Let's
talk superheroes.

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:42.264
You seem to have
an interest in them.

00:03:42.264 --> 00:03:44.558
Robert: You know, I just
love, as a director,

00:03:44.558 --> 00:03:46.393
touching different things.

00:03:46.393 --> 00:03:48.061
And I love fantasy.

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:51.398
And "Meteor Man" came about
because when my nephew

00:03:51.398 --> 00:03:55.193
was really little, I was
living--I live in LA, and my

00:03:55.193 --> 00:03:57.487
family, you know, he was
living in Chicago.

00:03:57.487 --> 00:03:59.739
And he was a little kid, he's
like six years old, and I said,

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:01.575
"For Halloween, who
are you going to be?

00:04:01.575 --> 00:04:03.118
You going to be Spiderman,
you going to be Batman?"

00:04:03.118 --> 00:04:06.163
And he says, "I can't be
them because they're white."

00:04:06.163 --> 00:04:07.831
And I was like--as a little kid.

00:04:07.831 --> 00:04:10.667
And I was like,
"No, I love everybody.

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:12.294
You could be whatever you want."

00:04:12.294 --> 00:04:15.005
And then when he said that,
it kind of hurt my heart,

00:04:15.005 --> 00:04:16.965
and I was like, "You know what?

00:04:16.965 --> 00:04:21.970
I'm going to create a superhero
of color to fill that void."

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.973
And then that's when I started
to write "Meteor Man" and come

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:27.893
up with the legend of, you know,
Meteor Man and, you know,

00:04:27.893 --> 00:04:29.686
making a superhero from scratch.

00:04:29.686 --> 00:04:33.648
Tracy: Were you to any degree
offended when people were like,

00:04:33.648 --> 00:04:36.485
"Yay, Black Panther, we finally
got a black superhero,"

00:04:36.485 --> 00:04:39.488
and you're like,
"Hello, Meteor Man."

00:04:39.488 --> 00:04:42.073
Robert: Well, you know, on
social media, people were,

00:04:42.073 --> 00:04:44.576
you know, defending me
and different things.

00:04:44.576 --> 00:04:47.037
"Robert Townsend been
in Wakanda for 25 years.

00:04:47.037 --> 00:04:49.998
What y'all talking about?"

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:51.875
But here's the thing,
here's the thing.

00:04:51.875 --> 00:04:55.253
When I see, you know,
Black Panther or Luke Cage

00:04:55.253 --> 00:04:58.798
or Black Lightning, those
are all my cinematic sons

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:02.427
and daughters, they really are,
because I know

00:05:02.427 --> 00:05:04.095
I planted a seed.

00:05:04.095 --> 00:05:06.848
And you know, when I
think about, you know,

00:05:06.848 --> 00:05:10.227
"Black Panther," you know,
making a billion dollars,

00:05:10.227 --> 00:05:12.896
you know, I was going for
a billion dollars back then.

00:05:12.896 --> 00:05:14.773
You know, it's just that
the audience didn't

00:05:14.773 --> 00:05:16.608
catch up to the vision.

00:05:16.608 --> 00:05:18.235
And so, when I created
Meteor Man,

00:05:18.235 --> 00:05:19.528
I had every celebrity.

00:05:19.528 --> 00:05:21.655
I had, you know, from
Naughty by Nature

00:05:21.655 --> 00:05:23.823
to Nancy Wilson to
Luther Vandross

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:29.204
to Robert Guillaume,
Marla Gibbs, Sinbad.

00:05:29.204 --> 00:05:31.331
You know, it was
an all-star cast.

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:35.043
So, you know, but I just feel
that, you know, I've always been

00:05:35.043 --> 00:05:38.755
a pioneer on certain levels,
where I try stuff that other

00:05:38.755 --> 00:05:40.382
people wouldn't.

00:05:40.382 --> 00:05:43.385
And sometimes, you know, you
get a little, you know--takes

00:05:43.385 --> 00:05:45.136
a moment for the audience to
catch up to you sometimes

00:05:45.136 --> 00:05:46.513
when you have that
kind of vision.

00:05:46.513 --> 00:05:50.559
Tracy: How do you
remain optimistic?

00:05:50.559 --> 00:05:53.353
Robert: You know, the thing
that I always say is that what

00:05:53.353 --> 00:05:58.900
our jobs are really about as
artists is to create change

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:00.443
through our art.

00:06:00.443 --> 00:06:03.154
Anytime you can get a chance
to say something, to create

00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:07.659
something, we--you know,
it's kind of like the fool

00:06:07.659 --> 00:06:09.286
in King Lear.

00:06:09.286 --> 00:06:11.955
You know, the fool says a lot of
good stuff and strong stuff, but

00:06:11.955 --> 00:06:16.293
he uses, you know,
honey to say it and use it.

00:06:16.293 --> 00:06:19.254
So, I think--you know, you can
get depressed or you can get sad

00:06:19.254 --> 00:06:22.090
by what's going on, but I tend
to look and I go,

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.592
"Look what happened.

00:06:23.592 --> 00:06:25.093
You know, people are getting--"
People said,

00:06:25.093 --> 00:06:26.678
"Oh, the Civil Rights movement
is over with.

00:06:26.678 --> 00:06:28.555
We don't have to do anything."

00:06:28.555 --> 00:06:30.682
You know, and it's like
I was getting an award

00:06:30.682 --> 00:06:32.350
with John Lewis.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.228
And I was sitting at this
table with John Lewis, and I was

00:06:35.228 --> 00:06:37.856
talking to him, and you know,
he said that,

00:06:37.856 --> 00:06:40.191
"The pendulum has swung back."

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:42.861
He said, "I thought
all of this was gone."

00:06:42.861 --> 00:06:44.988
And he says when he's seen
everything, he says,

00:06:44.988 --> 00:06:47.991
"I've got to dig my heels in now
and fight even harder."

00:06:47.991 --> 00:06:50.744
And I feel the same way as
an artist, as a filmmaker.

00:06:50.744 --> 00:06:53.455
Everything I've got to do, I've
got to sprinkle in my messages

00:06:53.455 --> 00:06:56.708
that will hopefully plant
other seeds in people

00:06:56.708 --> 00:06:57.959
that are listening.

00:06:57.959 --> 00:06:59.586
'Cause it's not about just being
funny or being stupid

00:06:59.586 --> 00:07:01.254
or what have you.

00:07:01.254 --> 00:07:03.298
It's as artists and especially
artists of color,

00:07:03.298 --> 00:07:05.842
I want to plant those
kind of seeds.

00:07:05.842 --> 00:07:07.802
You know, I want--you know,
knowing that the parenthood

00:07:07.802 --> 00:07:11.431
did help raise a generation of
babies to do positive things

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:13.850
and, you know, want to do
the right thing,

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:15.852
I feel really happy.

00:07:15.852 --> 00:07:18.438
There's other stories that I
want to tell, and it's about,

00:07:18.438 --> 00:07:21.399
you know, looking at tough times
and saying,

00:07:21.399 --> 00:07:23.526
"How can I make it better?"

